NAME
    touch - set a file's modification time, creating it when absent

SYNOPSIS
    touch [-c] [-d SECONDS] [-m] FILE...

DESCRIPTION
    Sets each FILE's modification time to the current wall-clock instant,
    creating an empty file when it does not exist.

    -c          Do not create a missing file; leave it absent without error.
    -d SECONDS  Use this exact instant, in whole Unix UTC seconds, instead of
                the clock's. There is no date-string parser and no timezone
                source, so the instant is given as seconds.
    -m          Select the modification time. This is the only time TROE's
                filesystem ABI records, so the option is accepted and
                redundant.

    Requires filesystem-read to test existence and filesystem-mutate to create
    or stamp, so it works on a writable volume such as /vol/root and is refused
    on the read-only root.

    A provider that records no modification time, such as the quota-bound /tmp,
    reports success without recording one: the file exists, which is what touch
    was asked for, and no filesystem refuses touch for this reason. See man ls
    for which listings show a time.

    A wall clock that is not yet set is a different, transient condition and is
    reported, because it would otherwise silently drop a time the filesystem
    could have recorded. Run timesync, or give -d an explicit instant.

    Every operand is attempted; a failure is reported and the remaining
    operands still run. A missing file with -c is not a failure.
